<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bishopric of Worms</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Prince-Bishopric of Worms, Diocese of Worms</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bishopric of Worms was a diocese on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, centred on the cathedral city of Worms, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> also ruled a small territory as princes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. In the High <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it held lands and rights that local lords such as the counts of Katzenelnbogen and Nassau administered or contested. Its territory was secularised in 1803.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bishopric-of-worms.jpg" alt="Bishopric of Worms" width="320" height="377" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicholas de Fer, 1705 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Worms_and_Speyer_Bishoprics_1705.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
